# === Boot shim — the kernel entry point (ELF64, Path C: gnoboot hands off here) ===
#
# Guarded by the build-time macro `ELF64_KERNEL`, which every kernel builder defines: scripts/build.sh
# and scripts/test.sh prepend `#define ELF64_KERNEL` (with CYRIUS_ELF64_KERNEL=1, the backend half), and
# bench.sh / ktest.sh build through build.sh. mbi.cyr's boot_info_capture_rdi sits under the same guard.
#
# ⭐ 1.57.12 — THE LEGACY ELF32 MULTIBOOT1 SHIM IS DELETED. It lived above this block under
# `#ifndef ELF64_KERNEL` (32-bit protected-mode entry, its own 0-4 MB page tables at 0x1000-0x4000, a
# 24-byte GDT at 0x4000, a far jump to long mode) and could not be built: no builder leaves ELF64_KERNEL
# undefined, and QEMU's `-kernel` loader refuses the ELF64 image outright (no PVH note — CLAUDE.md). It
# also carried two of the three fixed region-1 boot-stack immediates (`mov esp, 0x3E0000` at its step 1,
# `mov rsp, 0x3E0000` at its step 12). With the BSP boot stack now a .bss symbol (below), those two
# would have been the one place a fixed region-1 stack could come back unnoticed, so the dead path went
# with the move rather than being kept under a gate exemption. Its text is in git history (1.57.11 and
# earlier); the Path A background is agnosticos/docs/development/path-a-elf64-multiboot2.md. Its
# "step 12" note (the 0x200000 boot stack that grew into .rodata and overwrote "exfatu: upcase-checksum
# OK", found by a gdb watchpoint) is the first entry in the history at step 1 below.

#ifdef ELF64_KERNEL
# === 64-bit boot shim (ELF64 + sovereign-struct entry, Path C) ===
#
# Gnoboot (the AGNOS sovereign UEFI bootloader) hands control here
# in 64-bit long mode (paging on, UEFI identity-maps the first 4 GB,
# GDT inherited from UEFI, CS = 64-bit code segment, interrupts
# disabled). Per the AGNOS Path C handoff contract:
#   RDI = physical address of the AGNOS boot-info struct
#         (magic 0x41474E4F='AGNO'; layout in
#         agnosticos/docs/development/path-c-sovereign-uefi.md § Handoff)
#
# Pre-Path-C builds expected RAX=0x36d76289 + RBX=MBI per multiboot2
# § 8.4.3. That path is dead — GRUB's strict-W^X EFI relocator broke
# it irrecoverably (iron-nuc-zen log § Diagnosis 2). Path C drops GRUB
# entirely; gnoboot is the only thing between UEFI firmware and us.
#
# Sequence (minimal — just what's needed before kernel main can run):
#   1. Install our 64-bit stack: the 16-byte-aligned top of `bsp_boot_stack`, a 64 KB array in the
#      kernel image's own .bss (1.57.12 — a fixed region-1 window until then: top 0x3E0000 at 1.57.11,
#      0x3A0000 at 1.57.7-1.57.10, 0x380000 at 1.57.3-1.57.6, 0x200000 before). Done first because the
#      `boot_info_capture_rdi()` call after it uses the stack (call pushes the return address).
#   2. Init COM1 UART (port I/O; the sequence the deleted legacy shim's step 2 used, unchanged).
#
# Deliberately NOT done in this shim (let kernel-side init handle it):
#   - GDT install — `gdt_init()` in core/main.cyr (called via top-level
#     emit, see arch/x86_64/gdt.cyr) replaces UEFI's GDT with the full
#     7-entry kernel GDT (kCS/kDS/uDS/uCS/TSS×3). Until then, UEFI's
#     GDT stays live with CS=0x38 (some-UEFI-specific) and DS=0x18.
#     Trying to install a minimal 3-entry GDT here and switch via
#     retfq triple-faulted under QEMU OVMF (GRUB 2.12-9+deb13u2,
#     edk2-ovmf 202602-3, 2026-05-13). Letting `gdt_init` do it later
#     works because by that point the kernel's running its own code
#     with proper kernel-stack and can safely do the full switch.
#   - IDT install — `idt_init()` does it later
#   - Page tables — UEFI's identity map is fine
#   - CR0/CR3/CR4/EFER — UEFI configured them; kernel re-tunes later
#
# RDI is preserved through this minimal shim: the stack setup touches RAX/RCX (the
# `bsp_boot_stack_lo = &bsp_boot_stack;` statement below: movabs rax / movabs rcx / mov [rcx], rax),
# RFLAGS and RSP/RBP; the UART init touches RDX/AL. RDI intact for the boot_info_capture_rdi() call
# that follows. (RDI is a SysV scratch register, but `boot_info_capture_rdi()` takes no args, so
# cyrius doesn't clobber it at the call site.)
#
# ⭐⭐ 1.57.12 — THE BSP BOOT STACK IS AN IMAGE SYMBOL, NOT A FIXED WINDOW. `bsp_boot_stack` is 64 KB of
# the kernel's own .bss (a module-scope `var x[N]` is N u64, so N = 8192). Raw bytes cannot name a
# symbol and a top-level assignment can, so the shim loads the array's address through the one
# statement between its first two asm blocks, and the second block turns it into the stack top.
# WHY IT LEFT REGION 1 (operator ruling: make room for the 1.57.12 ZFS write path):
#   · Until 1.57.12 the stack was a fixed window above the image — top 0x3E0000, then a 64 KB guard gap,
#     then the BSP TSS.RSP0 window [0x3F0000, 0x400000) — and the image had to end at 0x3D0000. Every
#     image growth re-picked the number (top 0x200000 -> 0x380000 -> 0x3A0000 at 1.57.7 -> 0x3E0000 at
#     1.57.11): "a number above the image end of the day", the class gdt.cyr's AP-stack note counts three
#     failures of (the first one HERE: the 0x200000 stack grew down into .rodata and overwrote "exfatu:
#     upcase-checksum OK", found only by a gdb watchpoint). A symbol in the image moves WITH the image.
#   · gnoboot pins the whole image (one AllocatePages(AllocateAddress, EfiLoaderCode) sized from p_memsz)
#     and zeroes every [p_filesz, p_memsz) gap, so .bss is loader-owned and writable from instruction one,
#     on QEMU and on iron. The old window was safe only because the FIRMWARE left it free, which kmain
#     could report but never ensure. kmain now reports the stronger fact — `boot: BSP boot stack [lo, hi)
#     inside the loader-pinned image (EfiLoaderCode) OK` (main.cyr, mbi.cyr uefi_span_check); the
#     violation lines are in SMOKE_INVARIANT_DENY.
#   · It stays mapped under every CR3 kmain will run under: kmain is proc 0 and is switched out on this
#     stack under per-process CR3s, which all mirror PD[0..1] (proc_create_address_space), and no user
#     segment can override PD[1] (the ELF loaders refuse anything below 0x400000).
#   · Prior art: Linux x86_64 head_64.S `leaq __top_init_kernel_stack(%rip), %rsp` (init_stack inside
#     .data via INIT_TASK_DATA, vmlinux.lds.S); FreeBSD amd64 locore.S `movq $bootstack,%rsp` (.bss);
#     seL4 x86_64 head.S boot_stack_top (.phys.bss), then kernel_stack_alloc (.bss, src/kernel/stack.c);
#     vidya boot_and_startup: "Allocate a static boot stack in the kernel's .bss section (e.g., 64KB)".
# ⚠ COST — NO GUARD PAGE, SO A RED ZONE. The stack is inside the image's RW 2 MB identity mapping, so an
#   overflow would write the .bss global declared just before it, silently: in the 1.57.12 plain build that is
#   syscall.cyr's blk_rw_armed (48 B below the array; the raw-disk-write gate tests `blk_rw_armed == 0`, so
#   garbage ARMS it), BLK_RW_ARM_MAGIC, blk_rw_arm_pid, then the 4 KB blk_sc_bounce DMA bounce buffer — and the
#   neighbour changes with unrelated edits (it is whatever agnos.cyr's includes declare last before this file).
#   So the lowest 8 KB of the array is a RED ZONE (proc.cyr bsp_stack_redzone_arm / _check): main.cyr fills it
#   with a canary (Linux's STACK_END_MAGIC, doubled to a qword), and every exit from this stack — sched_leave_old
#   (each switch-out of kmain) and sched_assert_oob (the boot-only exec_and_wait / kernel_resume) — re-reads all
#   of it and PANICs + halts on one changed qword. 56 KB stay usable; scripts/check/image-layout-check.sh (7)
#   keeps every frame smaller than the zone, so a descent cannot step over it. A KSTACK_HW=1 build paints the
#   usable part and prints `kstack-hw: bsp boot stack max=0x... of 0xe000 usable` as it grows (proc.cyr);
#   measured peaks are in the 1.57.12 CHANGELOG entry, a few KB. A real guard page needs kmain moved onto a
#   region-7 slot once the direct map exists (FreeBSD's two-stack shape) — possible later, with this stack as its
#   early one; a not-present page HERE would mean splitting the kernel text's PD[1] into 4 KB pages.
# ⛔ THE STATEMENT AND THE BYTES ARE ONE UNIT. scripts/check/image-layout-check.sh (check.sh gate 34, and
#   build.sh after every x86_64 build) requires exactly one `48 B8 imm64` (movabs rax, &bsp_boot_stack),
#   `48 B9 imm64` (movabs rcx, &bsp_boot_stack_lo), `48 89 01` (mov [rcx], rax), `48 05 00 00 01 00`,
#   `48 83 E0 F0`, `48 89 C4`, `48 89 E5` in .text right after the CP1 stamp, with [imm64, imm64 + 0x10000)
#   inside .bss and the add immediate = 8 x this array's length. If cycc ever emits anything between the
#   statement and the second asm block, RAX is no longer the address and the build is refused.
var bsp_boot_stack[8192];   # 64 KB = 8192 u64; initial RSP = (&bsp_boot_stack + 0x10000) & ~0xF
var bsp_boot_stack_lo = 0;  # = &bsp_boot_stack once the shim has run (the statement below); main.cyr checks it
asm {
    # === Persistent CMOS boot-log checkpoint 1 (kernel entered) ===
    # Attempt 7 (visual canary) returned an ambiguous null-result. Single-
    # machine dev setup (devbox IS the iron target) means serial is wired
    # to nothing. CMOS scratch RAM (battery-backed, survives reset) is
    # readable from Linux after reboot — see agnosticos/scripts/read-boot-log.sh
    # (Cyrius binary using iopl(3) + outb/inb; /dev/nvram is unusable on
    # archaemenid because the BIOS CMOS checksum slot is left at 0x00).
    #
    # Layout (post-2026-05-14; slots shifted +0x10 from the original
    # 0x40-0x43 layout — archaemenid BIOS writes 0x42/0x43/0x44 every
    # cold boot, so the whole scheme moved into the virgin 0x50-0x7F
    # CMOS region):
    #   CMOS[0x50] = highest checkpoint reached this boot (1..N)
    #   CMOS[0x51] = magic (0xAB) — set once at entry to certify "kernel ran"
    #   CMOS[0x52] = gnoboot checkpoint (1..5)  — written by gnoboot
    #   CMOS[0x53] = gnoboot magic (0xCD)       — written by gnoboot
    #
    # Per-checkpoint write is 8 bytes: `mov al, idx; out 0x70, al;
    # mov al, val; out 0x71, al`. Clobbers AL only; RDI / RSP / all GPRs
    # except AL untouched. Bit 7 of port 0x70 controls NMI; we write
    # 0x50 / 0x51 (bit 7 clear → NMI enabled) — same state UEFI handed
    # off in, so no behavior change.
    0xB0; 0x51; 0xE6; 0x70; 0xB0; 0xAB; 0xE6; 0x71;        # CMOS[0x51] = 0xAB (magic)
    0xB0; 0x50; 0xE6; 0x70; 0xB0; 0x01; 0xE6; 0x71;        # CMOS[0x50] = 1    (entry)

    # (Boot-time visual canary removed v1.30.1 — shell now reaches iron
    # reliably; CMOS[0x51]=0xAB is the authoritative "kernel ran" signal,
    # and the white 256-pixel stripe at y=0..7 was overwriting the first
    # row of the kernel framebuffer log. The slot it occupied — CMOS[0x50]
    # = 2 ("past canary") — is gone too. Existing CP labels >= 3 are kept
    # at their current numeric values so read-boot-log keeps reading.)
}

# 1. 64-bit stack: the top of bsp_boot_stack (1.57.12 — the why is at its declaration above). The
#    statement compiles to `movabs rax, &bsp_boot_stack; movabs rcx, &bsp_boot_stack_lo; mov [rcx], rax`:
#    no stack use, RDI untouched, and the address left in RAX for the block below.
bsp_boot_stack_lo = &bsp_boot_stack;
asm {
    # REGISTER CONTRACT — in: RAX = &bsp_boot_stack (the statement above). out: RSP = RBP = the
    # 16-byte-aligned top (RAX + 0x10000) & ~0xF, inside the array. clobbers: RAX, RFLAGS (add/and);
    # the statement above clobbered RCX. preserves: RDI (gnoboot's &boot_info) and every other GPR.
    # The add immediate is the array's size in bytes (8 x 8192); image-layout-check.sh decodes it.
    0x48; 0x05; 0x00; 0x00; 0x01; 0x00;                    # add rax, 0x10000
    0x48; 0x83; 0xE0; 0xF0;                                # and rax, -16
    0x48; 0x89; 0xC4;                                      # mov rsp, rax
    0x48; 0x89; 0xE5;                                      # mov rbp, rsp

    # === Checkpoint 3 (past stack setup) ===
    0xB0; 0x50; 0xE6; 0x70; 0xB0; 0x03; 0xE6; 0x71;        # CMOS[0x50] = 3

    # 2. COM1 UART init (port 0x3F8) — the deleted legacy shim's step-2 sequence, unchanged
    0xBA; 0xF9; 0x03; 0x00; 0x00; 0xB0; 0x00; 0xEE;        # IER  = 0
    0xBA; 0xFB; 0x03; 0x00; 0x00; 0xB0; 0x80; 0xEE;        # LCR  = DLAB=1
    0xBA; 0xF8; 0x03; 0x00; 0x00; 0xB0; 0x01; 0xEE;        # DLL  = 1 (115200, low)
    0xBA; 0xF9; 0x03; 0x00; 0x00; 0xB0; 0x00; 0xEE;        # DLM  = 0 (115200, high)
    0xBA; 0xFB; 0x03; 0x00; 0x00; 0xB0; 0x03; 0xEE;        # LCR  = 8N1, DLAB=0
    0xBA; 0xFA; 0x03; 0x00; 0x00; 0xB0; 0xC7; 0xEE;        # FCR  = enable+clear FIFOs, 14-byte trigger
    0xBA; 0xFC; 0x03; 0x00; 0x00; 0xB0; 0x0B; 0xEE;        # MCR  = DTR | RTS | OUT2

    # === Checkpoint 4 (past COM1 init) ===
    0xB0; 0x50; 0xE6; 0x70; 0xB0; 0x04; 0xE6; 0x71;        # CMOS[0x50] = 4
}

# Capture RDI (sovereign boot-info ptr) into boot_info_ptr. Top-level
# call — emits a `call <fn>` instruction in source order. The fn takes
# no args, so cyrius doesn't touch RDI at the call site, and RDI from
# gnoboot survives into the body. Inside the function (mbi.cyr), local
# `var p = &boot_info_ptr;` loads RAX immediately; then asm
# `mov [rax], rdi` writes RDI to boot_info_ptr. See mbi.cyr for the why.
boot_info_capture_rdi();

# === CR4: CPUID-gated SMEP (bit 20) + SMAP (bit 21) enable, then CP 5 ===
#
# The legacy ELF32 multiboot1 shim (deleted at 1.57.12) enabled these on
# the way into long mode. The Path C handoff comes from gnoboot with PAE
# already on and SMEP/SMAP untouched — UEFI leaves both at 0 on
# archaemenid (Attempt 18 dump CMOS[0x54] = 0x00 confirmed). The comment
# in core/main.cyr that claimed "CR4.SMAP set in the boot shim" was
# stale w.r.t. Path C; without SMAP=1 here, the `stac` in the kernel's
# memory-isolation test (core/main.cyr ~line 471) is #UD → triple-fault
# → reset. That is the Attempt-16/17/18 stall profile exactly (kcp pins
# at 0x18, screen ladder dies past 0x12).
#
# Read-modify-write CR4 via EBX; CPUID leaf 7 sub-leaf 0 returns feature
# bits in EBX (SMEP at EBX[7], SMAP at EBX[20]) so we test against the
# *original* CPUID-EBX (stashed in EAX) before each set. Skips silently
# on QEMU `qemu64` (lacks both); enables both on Zen (advertises both)
# and any qemu `-cpu max`. Push/pop in long mode default to 64-bit
# (`0x53` = `push rbx`, `0x5B` = `pop rbx`) — fine because CR4's upper
# bits are all zero (no defined CR4 bit above 31 today).
#
# We then write CMOS[0x50] = 5 — combined "boot_info_capture_rdi
# returned AND CR4 SMEP/SMAP enabled". Avoids a CMOS-value collision
# with main.cyr's `CMOS[0x50] = 0x06` (GDT/TSS/IDT loaded). The cost is
# coarser bisection: if kcp pins at 4, the fault is in either
# boot_info_capture_rdi OR the CR4 block. CPUID/`mov cr4` from ring-0
# are routine, so the merged checkpoint is a reasonable trade.
asm {
    0x0F; 0x20; 0xE3;                                      # mov  ebx, cr4
    0x53;                                                  # push rbx                ; save working CR4
    0xB8; 0x07; 0x00; 0x00; 0x00;                          # mov  eax, 7
    0x31; 0xC9;                                            # xor  ecx, ecx
    0x0F; 0xA2;                                            # cpuid                   ; EBX = feature bits
    0x89; 0xD8;                                            # mov  eax, ebx           ; stash features in eax
    0x5B;                                                  # pop  rbx                ; restore working CR4
    0xA9; 0x80; 0x00; 0x00; 0x00;                          # test eax, 0x80          ; SMEP supported?
    0x74; 0x06;                                            # jz   +6                 ; skip OR if not
    0x81; 0xCB; 0x00; 0x00; 0x10; 0x00;                    # or   ebx, 0x100000      ; CR4.SMEP
    0xA9; 0x00; 0x00; 0x10; 0x00;                          # test eax, 0x100000      ; SMAP supported?
    0x74; 0x06;                                            # jz   +6                 ; skip OR if not
    0x81; 0xCB; 0x00; 0x00; 0x20; 0x00;                    # or   ebx, 0x200000      ; CR4.SMAP
    0x0F; 0x22; 0xE3;                                      # mov  cr4, ebx

    # === Checkpoint 5 (boot_info_capture_rdi returned AND CR4 done) ===
    # If CMOS[0x50] reads 5 after reset, kernel survived its first
    # call/ret + CR4 SMEP/SMAP enable — fault is somewhere later in
    # top-level kernel init (gdt_init, idt_init, paging, scheduler, ...).
    0xB0; 0x50; 0xE6; 0x70; 0xB0; 0x05; 0xE6; 0x71;        # CMOS[0x50] = 5
}
#endif
